NNAAMMEE
       ldattach - attach a line discipline to a serial line

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llddaattttaacchh [--11227788ddeennooVVhh] [--ii _i_f_l_a_g] [--ss _s_p_e_e_d] _l_d_i_s_c _d_e_v_i_c_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The llddaattttaacchh daemon opens the specified _d_e_v_i_c_e file (which should refer
       to a serial device) and attaches the line discipline _l_d_i_s_c to it for
       processing of the sent and/or received data. It then goes into the
       background keeping the device open so that the line discipline stays
       loaded.

       The line discipline _l_d_i_s_c may be specified either by name or by number.

       In order to detach the line discipline, kkiillll(1) the llddaattttaacchh process.

       With no arguments, llddaattttaacchh prints usage information.

LLIINNEE DDIISSCCIIPPLLIINNEESS
       Depending on the kernel release, the following line disciplines are
       supported:

       TTTTYY(00)
           The default line discipline, providing transparent operation (raw
           mode) as well as the habitual terminal line editing capabilities
           (cooked mode).

       SSLLIIPP(11)
           Serial Line IP (SLIP) protocol processor for transmitting TCP/IP
           packets over serial lines.

       MMOOUUSSEE(22)
           Device driver for RS232 connected pointing devices (serial mice).

       PPPPPP(33)
           Point to Point Protocol (PPP) processor for transmitting network
           packets over serial lines.

       SSTTRRIIPP(44); AAXX2255(55); XX2255(66)
           Line driver for transmitting X.25 packets over asynchronous serial
           lines.

       66PPAACCKK(77); RR33996644(99)
           Driver for Simatic R3964 module.

       IIRRDDAA(1111)
           Linux IrDa (infrared data transmission) driver - see
           http://irda.sourceforge.net/

       HHDDLLCC(1133)
           Synchronous HDLC driver.

       SSYYNNCC__PPPPPP(1144)
           Synchronous PPP driver.

       HHCCII(1155)
           Bluetooth HCI UART driver.

       GGIIGGAASSEETT__MM110011(1166)
           Driver for Siemens Gigaset M101 serial DECT adapter.

       PPPPSS(1188)
           Driver for serial line Pulse Per Second (PPS) source.

       GGSSMM00771100(2211)
           Driver for GSM 07.10 multiplexing protocol modem (CMUX).

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --11, ----oonneessttooppbbiitt
           Set the number of stop bits of the serial line to one.

       --22, ----ttwwoossttooppbbiittss
           Set the number of stop bits of the serial line to two.

       --77, ----sseevveennbbiittss
           Set the character size of the serial line to 7 bits.

       --88, ----eeiigghhttbbiittss
           Set the character size of the serial line to 8 bits.

       --dd, ----ddeebbuugg
           Keep llddaattttaacchh in the foreground so that it can be interrupted or
           debugged, and to print verbose messages about its progress to
           standard error output.

       --ee, ----eevveennppaarriittyy
           Set the parity of the serial line to even.

       --ii, ----iiffllaagg _v_a_l_u_e...
           Set the specified bits in the c_iflag word of the serial line. The
           given _v_a_l_u_e may be a number or a symbolic name. If _v_a_l_u_e is
           prefixed by a minus sign, the specified bits are cleared instead.
           Several comma-separated values may be given in order to set and
           clear multiple bits.

       --nn, ----nnooppaarriittyy
           Set the parity of the serial line to none.

       --oo, ----ooddddppaarriittyy
           Set the parity of the serial line to odd.

       --ss, ----ssppeeeedd _v_a_l_u_e
           Set the speed (the baud rate) of the serial line to the specified
           _v_a_l_u_e.

       --cc, ----iinnttrroo--ccoommmmaanndd _s_t_r_i_n_g
           Define an intro command that is sent through the serial line before
           the invocation of llddaattttaacchh. E.g. in conjunction with line
           discipline GSM0710, the command 'AT+CMUX=0\r' is commonly suitable
           to switch the modem into the CMUX mode.

       --pp, ----ppaauussee _v_a_l_u_e
           Sleep for _v_a_l_u_e seconds before the invocation of llddaattttaacchh. Default
           is one second.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

